WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.989
Right before you took the chief

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.249
executive role, you spent some time

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.389
as a driver. Can you tell us a

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.169
little bit about what that

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:04.949
experience was like?

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.329
The best thing about driving for

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.038
Lyft was that I got to meet great

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.049
riders. You know, my third

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.909
couple that I drove had just

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.929
come out of a restaurant when I

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.639
picked them up. This was their first

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.619
restaurant meal since Covid in San

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.699
Francisco. They had been very

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.319
cautious and they went to a

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.939
restaurant called Earnest.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.619
It had been open for maybe six

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.209
months. I had never heard of it.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.499
A week later, my wife and I went

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:25.919
there.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.689
And were there any sort of things

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.189
you noticed when you were a driver

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.989
that might result in sort of changes

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.239
to either product or process?

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.029
Oh a ton.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.159
I mean, again, from a riders

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.509
perspective, look, our launch today

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.489
around travel, it's like, let's

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.359
figure out a way to make things

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.219
really easy for for riders because

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.209
that's what they want. And then

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.399
drivers really want and enjoy

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.079
the control they have over their own

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:46.859
lives, right?

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.149
They can drive when they want.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.529
They don't have to ask someone for

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.789
permission. And so we're really

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:51.839
going to focus on some of those

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.309
basics to make sure that, you know,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.449
we want people Lyft loyal.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.279
We want people to say, "I've got

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.569
both apps, but I should have taken a

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.109
Lyft."

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.279
And you've worked with some very

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.389
influential people in tech.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.429
I mean, Bill Gates and Jeff Bezos.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.749
Is there any piece of advice that

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.009
either of them have given you that

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.719
you still hold on to now that you're

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.649
CEO of Lyft?

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.359
Yeah. I mean, I'll focus on Jeff

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.889
Bezos. He really set focus on the

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.299
customer. So, for example, what we

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.249
launched today is, you know, your

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.831
plane wheels touch down.

00:01:15.832 --> 00:01:17.399
The flight attendant says you can

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.289
turn your phone on and

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.149
all you have to do is open the Lyft

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.279
app. Answer one question:

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.629
Do you have baggage or not?

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.489
And then hit go and then we take

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.959
care of the rest so that by the time

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.669
you're at the curb, the car has

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:30.599
already arrived.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.649
And just think about that for a

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:32.999
second. Think of the stress that you

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.219
have as soon as your plane is there.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.569
Is my baggage here or not?

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.219
How am I going to get to the hotel?

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.299
What's going to happen next?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.349
You can outsource a lot of that

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.129
stress to us.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:41.999
That's what we want to do.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.619
And that really is Jeff Bezos.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.899
You know, sort of I'm just copying

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.429
his strategy. It Work pretty well

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.659
for Amazon, and I think it can work

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:49.649
pretty well for Lyft and our

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:50.650
customers.

